================================================================================
CM211-Low-2  NOT_RUN does not sit outside the pass and fail count; it FAILS the regression
================================================================================
The log's section 2 says the parked state is "not a pass and not a failure, and counted as neither". The last
clause is wrong, and it matters because it invites a reader to treat a NOT_RUN guard as harmless.

MEASURED IN THE CODE THAT DECIDES IT:

  gen_regress.py:754   bad = s["fail"] + s["timeout"] + s["not_run"]
  gen_regress.py:758   return 0 if bad == 0 else 2
So not_run is summed into the bad total with fail and timeout, and a non-zero bad total is exit 2. A NOT_RUN guard
therefore fails its regression, and gen_round refuses to index a round whose regression is not clean.

AND THE CASE IS REACHABLE, not hypothetical. The guard is a CHECK-TIER entry:

  guard tier 'check', measured False, names a manifest True
  so --tier check selects it: True
  and with --no-coverage there is no vdb, so no expectation check runs for it,
  so it is graded NOT_RUN and the regression exits 2 on the guard alone.

WHETHER A COVERAGE-OFF CHECK-TIER REGRESSION IS EXPECTED TO CARRY THE GUARD. RULED, not open: the Critic raised it,
Runtime recommended the answer below, and the Orchestrator adopted it (see the ruling row in
dv/auto_dv/work/orchestrator/TASKS.md at 19:2xZ). The reasoning is kept because a reader who meets the behaviour
needs to know it was chosen rather than inherited.

RULING: yes, carry it, and yes, let that regression exit non-zero on it.
1. The guard exists to make the failing direction permanent. A coverage-off run cannot exercise it, so the only two
   honest outcomes are "not judged" and "failed"; NOT_RUN counted into the bad total gives exactly that. A clean
   pass would be the false green the guard was built to prevent.
2. Excluding the entry from coverage-off runs is worse than failing, because a reader of that regression would not
   learn the guard had not run. A silent absence is the failure mode this whole line of work exists to remove.
3. Treating this NOT_RUN differently inside the bad total would key a regression's exit on a reason string, and the
   same NOT_RUN label is produced by a failed build, which must fail. Separating them properly needs a verdict of
   its own, which is a flow change with real blast radius and does not belong in a corrigendum.

THE COST IS SMALL, measured rather than asserted: running the check tier WITH coverage took 87 runs in about five
minutes of wall time on already-compiled builds. So the remedy for anyone wanting a clean check-tier gate is to run
it with coverage, not to change the flow.

THE ALTERNATIVE CONSIDERED AND NOT TAKEN: a distinct verdict for "a deferred red no stage could judge", excluded
from the bad total by its own name rather than by a reason match, with its own red and a case in the regression
module's self-test. It is rt37-shaped and is frozen behind round 1 by the same ruling.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR A MEASURED ROUND, measured rather than assumed, because the ruling only bites when coverage is
off. A round runs ONE regression invocation over the whole tier-full plan, and the round driver passes neither
--no-coverage nor --repro (zero occurrences of each), while the regression computes its coverage flag as
"not --no-coverage and not --repro". So coverage is ON for every run in a round, the pre-merge pass runs the
expectation check, and the guard grades RED-OK rather than NOT_RUN: it contributes zero to the bad total and cannot
refuse a round's indexing. There is no per-tier coverage choice inside a round to get this wrong.

What this file fixes either way is the log's claim that the parked state is counted as neither; that is wrong under
every option above.

================================================================================
CM211-Info-2  the build-input figure is of a commit the log does not name
================================================================================
The log's identity section gives "30 changed paths ... two non-inputs" without naming the HEAD the comparison ran
against, and the figure moves with HEAD. Measured now, against the build's own source commit 813994b3d483:

  build source commit: 813994b3d483
    against d8f5d99   d8f5d99eb79b  inputs 30  non-inputs 2
    against c8821a9   c8821a96bc01  inputs 30  non-inputs 2
    against 71c1c70   71c1c706f1be  inputs 30  non-inputs 3
    against 962d31f   962d31f82d3b  inputs 32  non-inputs 3
    against b105c09   b105c09655a4  inputs 34  non-inputs 3
So the log's 30 and two reproduce ONLY against d8f5d99 or c8821a9, which give the same delta. The guard log is
committed at b105c09, where the same comparison gives 34 inputs and three non-inputs, so a reader who re-runs it
from the commit the log lives in gets different numbers and concludes the log is wrong. It is not wrong; it is
unlabelled. The head-mode supplement, when the round HEAD is announced and the canonical red is produced, states
the HEAD beside the figure, and any future build-input figure in a log of mine does the same.

WHAT IS UNAFFECTED. The unhittability argument the figure supports does not depend on the count at all: it rests on
gen_fcov_groups.svh and gen_fcov_pkg.sv being byte-identical between the build's commit and the log's, which holds
at every commit above.

